{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794444791,"nonce":"a24k13sVokd3B391m+n9sveiB3w=","message":"Committed as cdbf56d, not pushed.\n\nThree of the scope document's commands.\n\n- `hxn check [hl] [cpp] [cppia] [--wasm]`: builds and runs the Haxe program on each runtime named, or on HashLink and hxcpp when none is and both are ready. Each run has to exit with 0, and what they print to standard output has to be the same, less carriage returns; the first line that differs is named. It closes the point above about a manifest check: before any build it compares `\u003clibrary\u003e.hxn.json` as committed with what the source gives and stops if they differ, since a build would overwrite it. A crate with no manifest yet is built and gets one.\n- `hxn doctor [--vm \u003ccommand\u003e]`: what is installed (cargo and whether it is nightly, haxe, the VM, hxcpp, ash, emcc, node), each of the five targets as ready or with what it needs, and what would make builds faster (sccache; mold on Linux, lld-link on Windows). It exits 0 when HashLink or hxcpp is ready.\n- `hxn build --timings`: `cargo build --timings` for the adapter crate.\n\n`run` is now two steps inside the command, building the program's command and running it, so `check` can capture what a run prints.\n\nWhere this differs from the scope document:\n- Its `check` also \"runs layout asserts\". hxn-abi's layouts are asserted by its own unit tests and by the C header's static asserts; a library has none of its own to run.\n- Its `doctor` checks for zig, for cross-compiling with cargo-zigbuild. hxn does not cross-compile, so it is not looked for.\n- `hxn dev`, the watch loop, is not built.\n\ntests/cli/run.sh runs `check` on its runtime, checks that a changed manifest is refused, and that `doctor` finds something ready.\n\nRun: tests/cli/run.sh on Ash (hybrid and jit), stock HashLink on the NUC, hxcpp, CPPIA, Ash wasm and Emscripten. On the NUC `doctor` reports hl ready and the others as needing hxcpp or a nightly toolchain, which is how that machine is. Not run: Windows and hxcpp on Linux.","files":null}]}